# Wire's webapp rendered chat messages through markdown-it with a custom
# fence `highlight` callback (src/script/util/messageRenderer.ts at the
# fix parent):
#
#     markdownit.set({
#       highlight: function (code): string {
#         const containsMentions = mentionEntities.some(mention => {
#           const hash = createMentionHash(mention);
#           return code.includes(hash);
#         });
#         if (containsMentions) {
#           // disable code highlighting if there is a mention in there
#           return code;                       // <-- unescaped
#         }
#         return hljs.highlightAuto(code).value;
#       },
#     });
#
# markdown-it embeds whatever the highlight callback returns into the
# rendered fence HTML verbatim -- its own fence renderer wraps the string
# without escaping it -- and the callback's `code` parameter is a slice of
# the received chat message, so a message whose fenced block contains a
# mention hash ships its own HTML to the victim's DOM: stored XSS (CWE-79).
# The fix wraps the branch's return in underscore's `escape()` and, hardening
# the same render path, passes function replacements to the two
# `String.replace(mentionHash, ...)` calls so `$&`-style replacement patterns
# in mention text cannot splice surrounding markup. The advisory names the
# vector: "Insufficient escaping in markdown 'code highlighting'". The
# vulnerable form is present at the fix parent and the remediation at the
# fix; the row's commit is a plain single-file fix commit.
#
# Held. The raw passthrough the fix removes is a *return expression* fact,
# and no binding can address either endpoint of it.
#
#   - `vyql graph` on the file shows the USG already knows the passthrough:
#     `highlight#param#code --FLOWS--> Name --FLOWS--> highlight#ret#`, and
#     the registration exists as a `markdownit.set` call node two lines
#     above. Neither endpoint is addressable. Return statements lower to
#     `analysis.function.return` pseudo-calls
#     (internal/extract/lowering/lowering.go:1251) that only
#     `functionReturnDecoratorAbsent` reads (internal/bindings/bindings_core.go:203),
#     and there is no emission target for a function's returned value; the
#     parameter route (`query param as param`,
#     internal/bindings/compile_binding.go:675) forbids a `where` clause,
#     accepts only `emit source at param`, and fires for exported params
#     under a profile that lists the concept -- it cannot name one
#     callback's parameter. And no edge joins the registration call to the
#     callback:
#
#       markdownit.set Call#1239 (messageRenderer.ts:132) has no FLOWS edge
#       to highlight#param#code or highlight#ret#, and highlight#ret# has no
#       outgoing edge at all, because the caller that consumes the return is
#       markdown-it's own fence renderer, outside the scanned tree.
#
#     The join is absent in both spellings. Passing the highlighter by name
#     (`markdownit.set({highlight: highlightFence})` where `highlightFence`
#     returns its parameter raw) reports exactly the same one label as the
#     inline literal -- the fifth test pins that -- so this is not the
#     by-name/inline split the tarteaucitron callback shape shows, where the
#     named spelling resolves end to end and only the inline one dies.
#   - `vyql match` on the file reports one markdown-it label only: the
#     existing `code.RenderedMarkup` source at `markdownit.render`'s result
#     (markdown_render_markup_source.vyql). It is identical at both
#     revisions and by-design safe here, because the instance runs
#     `html: false`: markdown-it escapes source HTML everywhere except the
#     one place this CVE lives, the highlight callback's return, which is
#     precisely the unaddressable endpoint. Nothing labels the callback: no
#     source at its `code` parameter, no sink at its return, and no
#     `core.HtmlEscape` on the underscore `escape` the fix adds (only the
#     dotted `_.escape`/`lodash.escape` forms are bound). Binding the bare
#     `escape` would not discriminate anything anyway: the fix parent already
#     carries two `escape(...)` calls in sibling closures inside the same
#     outer function, so any escape-family negation that reads text is
#     satisfied at both revisions.
#   - a presence-form binding is the only data-expressible route, and it is
#     priced and rejected: a markdown-it-gated, function-scoped binding
#     keying on "highlighter call present, no escape-family call" reports 3
#     context hits on the vulnerable file and 0 on the patched -- and 1 hit
#     on a file transcribed from markdown-it's own README example, the
#     canonical safe hljs integration, because `hljs.highlight(lang,
#     str).value` returns already-escaped markup and no token distinguishes
#     it from `return code`. Narrowing the probe to `highlightAuto`, this
#     repository's own spelling, escapes the README hit only by keying on the
#     vulnerable commit's vocabulary. The discriminator that would separate
#     them -- "the callback returns its own parameter rather than a
#     highlighter's output or a literal" -- is an identity fact about the
#     return expression, which is the same missing endpoint.
#   - a `.replace` replacement-string sink for the two hardening hunks is
#     also priced and rejected: the second-argument shape is ordinary code
#     everywhere (`str.replace(/x/, val)`), and neither hunk is the
#     advisory's vector.
#
# What is missing is a binding-language capability, not vocabulary: an
# emission target at a function's return value, plus the join from a
# registration call to the callback it names -- a new dataflow relation, not
# a localized correction. The join half is on file twice for JavaScript
# (the registered-callback entry and the no-endpoint-for-callback-parameters
# entry), and the closure-literal half of the same callee-provenance family
# was prototyped on its own checkout in this recheck span -- a per-lambda
# Return node joined onto a parameter-position call result -- and measured
# precise but insufficient, so the family is not one localized correction
# away from reporting this. Here that prototype would not even apply: no
# parameter-position invocation of the callback exists in the scanned code,
# so the join it adds has nothing to join onto.
#
# Fresh measurement on the current knowledge base, 3 x `--cache off` runs
# per revision, timeout 180, `--max-ram 6GB`:
#   - src/script/util (57 files): 7 findings + 4 review flags at both
#     revisions, byte-identical across all six runs; the only rows on this
#     file are two pre-existing VYQL-INJ-134 prototype-pollution rows at
#     messageRenderer.ts:98 and :120, present at both revisions.
#   - src/script (707 javascript files + 1 config): 198 findings / 52 flags /
#     55 at or above high at both revisions, with identical
#     fingerprint sets across all six runs. The tree's VYQL-INJ-004 rows
#     sit on unrelated files (newVersionHandler.ts, MessageQuote.tsx,
#     WarningsContainer.tsx and friends) and none names this file or the
#     markdown-it render path through it.
#   - `vyql resolve` on the file: 87 call sites resolved, 87 unresolved.
#     `vyql explain`: the two INJ-134 rows and nothing else.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-engine-gap, all five tests rejected.
# When a return-value emission target and the registration-to-callback join
# land with a markdown-it highlight binding, the first and fifth tests flip
# to expect VYQL-INJ-004 while the third and fourth must stay reject (the
# first and third are the minimal pair: same registration, same
# highlighter, differing only in the returned expression; the fourth is the
# documented safe integration any presence rule must not flag).

test "rank1956: the mention-aware fence highlighter returns message content unescaped"
  lang javascript
  file src/script/util/messageRenderer.ts
  reject VYQL-INJ-004
  reject_review code.RenderedMarkup
  code
  ```
  import hljs from 'highlight.js';
  import MarkdownIt from 'markdown-it';

  const markdownit = new MarkdownIt('zero', {breaks: true, html: false, linkify: true});

  export const renderMessage = (message, mentionTexts) => {
    markdownit.set({
      highlight: function (code) {
        const containsMentions = Object.keys(mentionTexts).some(hash => code.includes(hash));
        if (containsMentions) {
          // disable code highlighting if there is a mention in there
          // highlighting will be wrong anyway because this is not valid code
          return code;
        }
        return hljs.highlightAuto(code).value;
      },
    });
    return markdownit.render(message);
  };
  ```

test "rank1956: the patched highlighter escapes the branch the fixed revision touches"
  lang javascript
  file src/script/util/messageRenderer-fixed.ts
  reject VYQL-INJ-004
  code
  ```
  import hljs from 'highlight.js';
  import MarkdownIt from 'markdown-it';
  import {escape} from 'underscore';

  const markdownit = new MarkdownIt('zero', {breaks: true, html: false, linkify: true});

  export const renderMessage = (message, mentionTexts) => {
    markdownit.set({
      highlight: function (code) {
        const containsMentions = Object.keys(mentionTexts).some(hash => code.includes(hash));
        if (containsMentions) {
          // disable code highlighting if there is a mention in there
          return escape(code);
        }
        return hljs.highlightAuto(code).value;
      },
    });
    return markdownit.render(message);
  };
  ```

